+`base-scheduler-native` ports Socketry's default work-stealing executor and `async-io` readiness backend from revision `7a70586d5d37fb640d696d0c03a05b2ee3cbe06b`. Worker-local queues, preferred-worker remote inboxes, idle work stealing, external fairness, and the publication/parking fence remain. This branch experiments with alternating ready batches, as described under worker ownership below. Before another ready batch runs, the worker makes a nonblocking driver turn to publish available completions. A driver can space those turns by task polls through `SelectorWorker::nonblocking_interval`: with an OS poller, the epoll and kqueue readiness worker turns at the first batch boundary after 61 polls, since each of its nonblocking turns is a system call, while io\_uring keeps publishing after every batch. When its queues are empty, the normal idle turn prepares and waits directly without a preceding nonblocking probe. Internal worker identity exists only for queue locality and blocking-wait detection; operations never find an implicit current scheduler. The root future can borrow local state and is polled on the caller's thread by `block_on`.
 
 The adapter adopts Base's owning task handles, guarded user destructors, lazy cancellation, and asynchronous shutdown. A short admission lock serializes spawning with closure; no task-ID registry is needed. Separate counters track task-future ownership and queued or running runnables: shutdown needs both to retire, since destroying a task future can precede the return of its final runnable. Runnable quiescence notifications arm when the first drain wait registers, so ordinary dispatch avoids notifying an unused event. Arming shares the runnable counter's modification order and remains enabled thereafter, preserving concurrent and abandoned waits. Closing admission preserves live task owners and wakes socket and clock operations. Drop does not join. Explicit shutdown drains tasks, accepted blocking/file/resolver work, cleanup, timers, and signal delivery. Applications coordinate cooperative cancellation and await tasks before shutdown. A task must not await shutdown of its own scheduler.
 
